{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 329,
    "dDurationMs": 3782,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that something is up with the way the universe is expanding - there’s some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4111,
    "dDurationMs": 5289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of anti-gravitational effect that’s\ncausing the expansion to accelerate. We don’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9400,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know what it is - just that it competes against the inward-pulling effect of gravity. And"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14100,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s winning - it looks like the universe\nwill expand forever, at an ever-increasing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18640,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate. We call this mysterious influence dark energy, but while we’ve talked a lot about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how it behaves, we’ve never really explored what it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28480,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, what is dark energy, really?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36680,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mainstream physical explanation for dark energy is that the vacuum of space has a constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42790,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy density. Empty space buzzes with random activity that we sometimes describe as virtual"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48300,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles popping into and out of existence, driving accelerated expansion. But there are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53870,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough problems with this that we need to\nexplore other options for what dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59470,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might be, and how it might behave. We’ve already mentioned one of the alternative behaviors:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65510,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the case where dark energy gets more intense over time rather than having a constant density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70430,
    "dDurationMs": 5930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expected of a vacuum energy. That would cause the universe to eventually tear itself apart"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a subatomic scale in the so-called big rip. But we never talked about what might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82320,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cause dark energy to behave this way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85250,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The default model for dark energy is that it can be described with a so-called cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90500,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant. This is just a static number that\nyou can add to Einstein’s equations of general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95770,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity to represent the fabric of space\nhaving a constant, non-zero energy density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101830,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the fabric of space has energy - dark energy\n- then the expansion of the universe creates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107790,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the stuff. And it’s this process that actually\naccelerates the expansion, which in turn accelerates"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114280,
    "dDurationMs": 5881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the creation of dark energy, and so on, ultimately leading to exponential acceleration. Exactly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120161,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "why the creation of dark energy accelerates expansion requires a dive into the hairy math"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125690,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of general relativity - and we tried that\nin a couple of previous episodes. For today,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132040,
    "dDurationMs": 7630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I need to ask you to accept that the anti-gravitational effect of dark energy is due to it having"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139670,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative pressure. That’s counter-intuitive\nbecause negative pressure is an inward pulling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145000,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure. But in an expanding universe negative\npressure actually energizes the expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151580,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another slightly counter-intuitive thing about dark energy is that, on top of the anti-gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 156700,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect of its negative pressure, it also produces regular attractive gravity due to its positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162620,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy density, just like regular matter and energy do. But the effect of its negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167530,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pressure is more powerful, so the net effect is antigravitational. We can describe the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173550,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“power” of any dark energy candidate by\ntaking the ratio of pressure to density. This"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179160,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gives us the equation of state of dark energy, with the ratio equated to the equation of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185890,
    "dDurationMs": 6161,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state parameter, omega. The value of omega tells you almost everything about what your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192051,
    "dDurationMs": 5929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark energy candidate will do to the universe. For dark energy, omega is negative due to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197980,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the negative pressure on the top of the fraction and the positive energy density on the bottom."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202840,
    "dDurationMs": 6399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Standard “cosmological constant” dark\nenergy has omega -1. This is just what you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209239,
    "dDurationMs": 3931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get when you say that the vacuum has a constant energy density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213170,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we assume this is true we can figure out\nwhat energy density would be needed to cause"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217750,
    "dDurationMs": 7210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the acceleration we observe. That number is around 5x10^-10 Joules/m^3. To give you a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 224960,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sense of the smallness of this, if you could\ntake all the dark energy in a space the size"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230170,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the earth and convert it to mass by E=mc^2, you’d only get about a grain of sand’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236810,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "worth of matter. That’s such a tiny amount\nthat it doesn’t have any real effect in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240980,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "places where there’s even a smattering of\nregular matter - like inside our solar system,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245910,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or even inside our galaxy. But if you include\nthe vast voids between the galaxies, the extremely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253370,
    "dDurationMs": 5649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diffuse dark energy adds up and ultimately\nthis becomes the dominant form of energy in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259019,
    "dDurationMs": 6351,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our universe. So its antigravitational effect\nnot only overcomes its own positive gravity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265370,
    "dDurationMs": 5761,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also the positive gravity of all other\nmatter. And it’s only getting started. As"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271131,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe gets bigger and galaxies get\nfurther and further apart, their gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275680,
    "dDurationMs": 5989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "connection dilutes away, while dark energy\njust keeps pushing and pushing and pushing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281669,
    "dDurationMs": 6291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we can measure the energy density, can’t we also measure omega, the equation of state?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287960,
    "dDurationMs": 7919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, yeah, and it’s pretty much … -1,\nthe default option. Our most precise measurement"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295879,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comes from Planck satellite data, which mapped the cosmic microwave background. The Planck"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301199,
    "dDurationMs": 11851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "team measured w = −1.028 ± 0.032. So, while this is perfectly consistent with omega=-1,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313050,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "there’s still some wiggle room for omega not quite equal to -1."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318169,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if evidence is pointing to omega=-1, and we have a plausible physical explanation for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324059,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "omega=-1, why should we spend youtube episodes and embarrassingly meager funding of theoretical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330699,
    "dDurationMs": 6930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physicists exploring other options? Well,\nI already mentioned that there are problems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337629,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the explanation where dark energy is\ndue to quantum fluctuations. For example, it’s actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342830,
    "dDurationMs": 7979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very difficult to get the vacuum energy to be very close to zero but not quite equal to zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350809,
    "dDurationMs": 7021,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A naive calculation for the energy of the\nvacuum gives you a number something like 120"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357830,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orders of magnitude larger than the measured energy density of dark energy. You can reduce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363650,
    "dDurationMs": 6669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that number if the quantum fields sort of cancel each other out. A perfectly symmetric canceling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370319,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could get you down to a vacuum energy of zero, but it’s really really hard to cancel such a large number"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377779,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down to nearly zero but not quite zero. This is the cosmological constant problem, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383729,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ve discussed it previously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385319,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A second reason is the so-called Hubble tension.When we measure the Hubble constant - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390610,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "current rate of expansion of the universe,\nbased on supernova explosions over the past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395310,
    "dDurationMs": 6559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several billion years, we get one number for that expansion rate. But if we calculate the current expansion"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401869,
    "dDurationMs": 5811,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rate based on observations of the very early universe we get a different number. The latter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is from Planck satellite measurements of the cosmic microwave background. The current thinking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412960,
    "dDurationMs": 5609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is that one of these measurements has a very subtle error. But it could also be that our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418569,
    "dDurationMs": 7050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assumed cosmology is wrong. In order to calculate the Hubble constant from the early universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425619,
    "dDurationMs": 5411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measurements, we also need to assume an equation of state to see how the expansion rate should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431030,
    "dDurationMs": 5379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have evolved into the late universe. The Planck measurement assumes a constant dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436409,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and an unchanging omega of -1. But if dark energy has changed over time, both teams may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443759,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have got the right answer and the discrepancy between their results could actually tell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448120,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us something about what dark energy really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451120,
    "dDurationMs": 6199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there’s a final reason to look for a\nmechanism for accelerating expansion due being some other"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457319,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than quantum fluctuations. It’s because we know there must be one to explain cosmic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464219,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inflation. This was a period of extreme exponential expansion that likely occurred during the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470749,
    "dDurationMs": 5950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big bang. That expansion must have been due to one or more quantum fields being in a highly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476699,
    "dDurationMs": 5831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energetic state, rather than all quantum fields fluctuating a teensy bit above their energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482530,
    "dDurationMs": 6499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "minima. So if a specific field was responsible for inflation, couldn’t one also be responsible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489029,
    "dDurationMs": 6521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for dark energy? The answer is yes, one could - and if that’s true then a lot of problems"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495550,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with cosmological constant dark energy could be solved. There are a few options for dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501840,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy as a new quantum field. Perhaps the most prominent is quintessence, proposed by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507990,
    "dDurationMs": 6650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Robert Caldwell, Rahul Dave and Paul Steinhardt in 1998, the same year as the acceleration"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514640,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe was discovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516600,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The name quintessence comes from Medieval Latin, and means ‘fifth essence.’ The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521229,
    "dDurationMs": 4611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alchemists thought everything in the world\nwas made of the four Aristotlean elements,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525840,
    "dDurationMs": 6510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "water, earth, fire, and air, while another\nfifth element called quintessence filled the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 532350,
    "dDurationMs": 7960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "celestial spheres beyond the earth. It’s\nan apt because it's field fills all of space and also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540310,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can be thought of as another force on top of the commonly known 4 fundamental forces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545610,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alternatively, it can be thought of as a fifth\nenergetic component of the universe on top"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550120,
    "dDurationMs": 2930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of baryons, dark matter, neutrinos, and photons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553050,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quintessence field would have to be a\nscalar field, like the Higgs field. So, it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558590,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would take on a simple numerical value - a\nfield strength - everywhere in space. The"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563590,
    "dDurationMs": 5381,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "equation of state depends on this field strength and the kinetic energy of the particles of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 568971,
    "dDurationMs": 7289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field. The field strength can also change over time AND over space, so omega can change,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576260,
    "dDurationMs": 6310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and with it the behavior of dark energy. This dynamical nature of quintessence is what makes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582570,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it so powerful. For example, if the strength\nof dark energy has changed since the early"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587590,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe then the Hubble tension could be\nexplained."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591790,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quintessence can also help solve some of the uncomfortable coincidences that seem necessary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596680,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a cosmological constant dark energy.\nCurrently around 70% of the energy in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602160,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe is dark energy with the remaining\n30% mostly matter, including dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608750,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That doesn’t sound very close, but it actually is. As the universe expands, matter dilutes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 615010,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away while most versions of dark energy stay constant or relatively constant, or even increase"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621770,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some models. The universe will spend the vast, vast majority of both its past and future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628130,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "history with a huge difference between the\ndensities of dark energy and matter. So why do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634300,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we happen to live in a period of the universe where these are within a factor of a few of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640600,
    "dDurationMs": 8010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other, at exactly the time when the acceleration begins? This could be a crazy coincidence,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648610,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or perhaps there are anthropic arguments for this - for example we could only exist in such a time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655690,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But quintessence actually gives us another\nexplanation. The quintessence field could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661660,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be coupled to the quantum fields responsible for radiation and matter, and its behavior"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666720,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could be connected to the density of the universe. For example, in so-called k-essence models,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673320,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the equation of state is connected to the density of matter. And it only"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677200,
    "dDurationMs": 6270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes dark-energy-like when matter starts to thin out. That provides a natural explanation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683470,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for why dark energy kicked in at around the same time as stars and planets were able to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689560,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "form. This same “tracker” behavior could\nalso help solve the cosmological constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695660,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem. If quintessence shifts to match the matter fields, it could potentially cancel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701010,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out their predicted extremely high vacuum\nenergy in a natural way, so that dark energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706730,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes the tiny residual from this canceling."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711070,
    "dDurationMs": 8140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so quintessence seems promising. Let’s look at how it actually behaves. As I mentioned,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719210,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the behavior of dark energy is driven by its\nequation of state. Any value of omega less"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724320,
    "dDurationMs": 10460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than -⅓ means accelerating expansion, and omega -1 is a constant energy density. Quintessence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734780,
    "dDurationMs": 7310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is often used to refer to any omega between -⅓ and -1, while values less than -1 are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742090,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usually called phantom energy. However the labels are a little bit ambiguous, and all of these could"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748470,
    "dDurationMs": 3230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "result from a new scalar quantum field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751700,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If omega is between -⅓ and -1 then the outward-pushing negative pressure still dominates over the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758530,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inward pull of regular gravity, so expansion\nstill accelerates - but that expansion is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765090,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "restricted to regions outside galaxies. The\nMilky Way survives a quintessence-dominated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771220,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe. That’s not true if omega is less than -1 - that’s the big rip, in which all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777760,
    "dDurationMs": 7170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "points in space eventually become infinitely far apart. Fortunately, that seems unlikely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784930,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The only way to get omega less than -1 is for the kinetic energy of the field to be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790950,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative. These sorts of negative energy scenarios break the rules in general relativity in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796910,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same way that time travel does. So, if the big rip is possible, so are time machines,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803790,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we escape the end of the universe every time it comes up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807600,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quintessence is an extremely flexible theoretical mechanism. There’s even a scenario in which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 813440,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the field evolves in such a way to halt the\nexpansion of the universe and cause it to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818820,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "collapse back on itself. But this flexibility\nmakes it hard to actually falsify the theory"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824520,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it’s just too easy to come up with a behavior of the field that fits our observations. That"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830700,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "said, physicists are trying. The most direct\ntest would be to find the particles of this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836140,
    "dDurationMs": 5030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field, for example in one of our particle\ncolliders. But there hasn’t been a whiff of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841170,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them yet, but that doesn’t tell us much.\nIf we could actually confirm a change in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 846650,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "density of dark energy then it would be strong support for quintessence, because that would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852460,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refute the cosmological constant model, and quintessence is its main competitor. The now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 858070,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fully operational James Webb Space Telescope will help by pushing our measurements of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion rate back billions of years from\nthe current supernova measurements. There"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869000,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are also suggestions that the quintessence field may cause a characteristic signature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874000,
    "dDurationMs": 7590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the stuff of the cosmic microwave background clumps together. But again, no firm conclusions yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881590,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long story short - measuring the equation\nof state of the universe with increasing precision"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887400,
    "dDurationMs": 7470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will teach us about its fabric, its origin,\nand its fate. Either the cosmological constant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894870,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem is coincidental with a quintessentially consistent dark energy, or scalar quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901570,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fields shift in a quintessence-saturated space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905830,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’re going to skip comment responses this week and double up next time. But I did want"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910620,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to take a minute to thank those of you who\nsupport us on Patreon. This show really is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915180,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supported in large part by the community.\nThat’s what gives us the freedom to really"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920540,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "listen to feedback and make the show that\nyou guys want. Now today I’d like to give an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926840,
    "dDurationMs": 4589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "extra special shoutout to Zachary Wilson, who’s supporting us at big bang level. Zachary, you are in many ways like quintessence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931429,
    "dDurationMs": 3591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zachary you are in many ways like quintessence, the fifth element. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935020,
    "dDurationMs": 6778,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the alchemists, the aether. In that you give us energy in the vacuum of the YouTube funding models. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941798,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Helping us exponentially expand our awesomeness or at the very least "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945568,
    "dDurationMs": 3823,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preventing Space Time collapsing in on itself. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949391,
    "dDurationMs": 747,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, thank you!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
